      "observed": "D2 (PMEG3030EP) is drawn vertically between the +5V arrow (top) and the servo power rail (bottom), with the cathode bar at the top, i.e. anode toward the servo rail and cathode toward +5V. In this orientation the diode conducts from the servo rail back into +5V and blocks +5V from reaching the servo rail.",
      "expected": "Per the spec, D2 is a 'Diode from +5V into the servo power rail (feeding J3/J8 pin 2 and the C1–C4 bank)'; its anode must connect to +5V and its cathode to the servo rail (the same forward direction as D1, whose cathode bar correctly faces the +5V rail).",
      "evidence": [
        {"source": "schematic", "quote": "D2 PMEG3030EP '3A 360mV' drawn with cathode bar toward the +5V arrow above it, anode side toward the rail feeding C1/C2/C3/C4 and J3 pin2 / J8 pin2 (sheet m5-pantilt.kicad_sch, row B/C, column ~3-4)"},
        {"source": "spec", "quote": "D2 | PMEG3030EP Schottky, schematic note \"3A 360mV\" | Diode from `+5V` into the servo power rail (feeding J3/J8 pin 2 and the C1–C4 bank)"}
      ],
      "impact": "The servo power rail would never be powered from +5V (neither from the M5Stack bus nor the external 5V input), so the servos on J3/J8 would not run; the diode instead provides a backfeed path from the servo rail into the +5V rail.",
      "confidence": 0.55
    }
  ],
  "summary": "All resistor/capacitor values, part identities, jumper annotations and the PWM/serial/battery/external-5V topology match the specification, but D2 appears drawn with reversed polarity (cathode toward +5V), which would prevent the servo power rail from being fed — a single polarity anomaly."
}

